#!/usr/bin/env sh
# Pre-commit gate — read this before trusting a green run.
#
# The promise: a green hook means this commit will not take `pnpm run ci` red
# for a reason the hook could have seen. It is not "CI ran".
#
# COVERS
#   1. typecheck            whole workspace, turbo-cached (runs on any staged file,
#                           so a lone tsconfig.json edit is still checked)
#   2. eslint               staged files under the linted roots, --max-warnings 0
#                           (warnings included: unused bindings are how five
#                           shipped-inert features were found in apps/web)
#   3. vitest related       specs whose module graph reaches a staged file
#   4. source-scraping specs  the eight tests that read repo files as text
#                           (eslint.config.mjs, globals.css, package.json).
#                           Vitest's module graph cannot see those reads, so
#                           `related` never selects them — they run every time
#   5. repo ratchets        the eight pure-file-reader gates from `pnpm run ci`
#                           (mobile placeholders/distribution, portal edge,
#                           handoff colours, ui token parity, studio dialect,
#                           feature reachability, css scales)
#
# DOES NOT COVER — CI still owns these, do not read green here as "safe to push"
#   - the full unit suite. Only related + scraping specs run; a change with a
#     non-obvious blast radius can still break a spec CI will catch
#   - web:check-bundle-size. It runs a full Next build, minutes per commit. A
#     hook that slow gets bypassed with --no-verify, which protects nothing
#   - check:traceability, Playwright e2e, and pnpm install --frozen-lockfile
#   - anything in apps/mobile, which is ESLint-ignored repo-wide
#
# History: until 2026-08-22 this hook ran typecheck only. An ESLint refactor was
# committed behind a green hook with a red unit test, and CI caught what the
# hook should have. If you widen or narrow the scope, update this list in the
# same change — a hook whose documented scope is wrong is worse than no hook,
# because people stop reading it.
pnpm exec lint-staged
